QoS

Categorize and identify types of traffic needing priority
Determine how much bandwidth is required
Efficiently use WAN link's bandwidth
Identify types of traffic to drop during network congestion

Delay - Time packet travels from source to desitination (ms)
Jitter - Uneven arrival of packets which is bad for VoIP traffic
Drop - When a router's interface queue overflows and causes packet loss

CATEGORIZATION
Grouped by buckets and we assign priority to them depending on which one should be faster and which one should be slower

Voice over IP service -> HIGH priority

Web browsing/Email -> LOW Priority

Ways to categorize

Best Effort - No QoS
    Traffic is first in, first out

Integrated Services (IntServ) - Hard QoS
    Traffic has strict bandwidth reservations (LIKE dedicated percentages)

Differentiated Services (DiffServ) - Soft QoS
    Routers and switches can make decisions based on marking and can fluctuate traffic (Percentages, but can increase or decrease depending on demand from that category)


MECHANISMS
Classification - Traffic is placed into different categories (like for email, POP3, IMAP, SMTP, Exchange) ; Does not alter any bits in the frame or packet
Marking - Alter bits wihtin frame ; Makes decisions based on markings
Congestion Management - Queueing (Empties packets in specified sequence and amount)
    Weighted Fair - Takes one from each category at a time
    Low-Latency - Takes all of one category at a time depending on priority
    Weight Round Robin (Hybrid) - The concept is it takes 3 out of highest priority, two out of second highest, and one out of the rest and repeat

CONGESTION AVOIDENCE
    Like a bucket, if you put more after maximum, it will overflow

    Random Early Detection (RED) - Prevent overflow from occuring ; Drops traffic based off priority


When putting it all together, we get into the concepts of Policing and Shaping

Policing - Discard packets exceeding the configured rate limit ; Dropped packets result in retransmission resulting in more bandwidth ; Only good for higher-speed interfaces

Shaping - Allows buffer to delay traffic from exceeding the configured rate ; Holds in buffer instead of dropping them ; Pushes them into empty space when available ; Better for slower speeds

Link Efficiency : 
    Compression = Packet payload is compressed to conserve bandwidth ; 
        VoIP Example : |IP| |UDP| |RTP| |Voice Payload| -> |cRTP| |Voice Payload / 2|

    Link Fragmentation and Interleaving (LFI)
        Fragments large data packets and interleaves smaller data packets between the fragments
        |V| |V| |V| |Data| --> |V| |D| |V| |D| |V| |D|